WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.601
-I just want to say
happy birthday to you.

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:03.767
Your birthday was yesterday.
-Yesterday, man.

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:04.934
-Happy birthday!
-Thank you, brother.

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:06.100
-You're the best.
Come on, man

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.434
-Thank you.
-Best of the best.

00:00:08.434 --> 00:00:10.467
Good luck
and many, many, many more years.

00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:11.767
-Thank you.
-Did you have fun?

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.100
-Yes.
You know, I chilled.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.767
Last year was 50, so it's 51,
so last year we did a big one.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:19.968
This year --
-What?

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:21.133
-I know, baby.

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.767
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:25.801
I'm joking, I'm joking.
-Wow!

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:27.734
-But, like, last year
was a big blowout,

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:29.667
so this year, quiet was nice.

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:31.634
Like, a small dinner,
play some golf.

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:33.200
I always celebrated
with a friend of mine,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.501
Peter Hochfelder.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:36.200
And he turned 60, and I was 50,

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.133
so last year,
we did a 110th birthday.

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:39.300
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.234
-So this year we just keep on,
112th this year.

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:44.834
Whatever, but we just chilled.
It was nice.

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:47.601
-Are you having
a good season here?

00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:50.868
Because the Giants are doing --
Well, I know they lost.

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:52.133
-Oh, they lost,
but they've won more games

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:53.334
than I thought
they were gonna win,

00:00:53.334 --> 00:00:55.133
so it's a great year.
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:56.834
-Everyone's kind of happy
in New York.

00:00:56.834 --> 00:00:58.000
-Yeah.
-Even the Jets.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.901
-The Giants are winning.
The Jets are winning.

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:03.200
Even when they lose,
people are still wearing gear.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.033
People are wearing stuff --
I haven't seen a Giants hat

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:08.234
or shirt or jacket in 10 years.

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:09.334
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:10.501
Everyone's psyched.

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:12.467
-No one's asked me
for a ticket in 10 years.

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:13.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.901 --> 00:01:15.968
And now my own kids are like,
"Can I get tickets to the game?"

00:01:15.968 --> 00:01:18.968
I'm like, "You didn't care.
I even played!"

00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:21.934
-"I was one of the greatest
Giants to play the game ever."

00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:24.133
-But it's good to see
the excitement back.

00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:26.267
I think -- you know, football
is so unpredictable.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:27.400
That's what I love about it.
-Yeah.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.033
-So unpredictable.
-Well, you know better

00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:31.267
than anyone New York is
a pretty tough place to play.

00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:32.400
-Oh, yeah.
-I mean, when you win,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.501
you're the top of the world,
when you lose...

00:01:35.501 --> 00:01:37.467
-You're under the shoe.
-Yeah.

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:38.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.234
Did you ever get that
when you were playing here?

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:43.100
-Oh, yeah, man.
Fans here are brutal.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.434
-Yeah.
-They tell you how they feel.

00:01:44.434 --> 00:01:47.033
They don't --
"Oh, it'll be next time."

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:48.734
No, no, no.
"You sucked yesterday."

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:49.734
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:51.133
-"You suck."
-"You suck."

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:53.667
-And to the point where
you're afraid to go out to eat.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.400
-Really?
-Oh!

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.834
When we lost,
I didn't want to go out to eat.

00:01:56.834 --> 00:01:58.868
I didn't even want to go
to the drive-through.

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:00.801
They would be like, "Here,
take your burgers, you punk!"

00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:02.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.601 --> 00:02:05.400
-"See if --" yeah.
"See if you catch this."

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.133
-"I'll cook at home."
-Yeah.

00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:10.367
You're known as one of
the greatest defensive players

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:11.734
of all time, but you also --

00:02:11.734 --> 00:02:12.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
Sure.

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:15.467
-Thank you.
-Of all time.

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:17.767
Of all time.
-I just -- yeah, it's hard

00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:20.100
to even hear, but...
-But you also scored

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.567
three touchdowns
throughout your career.

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:24.167
-Yeah, yeah.
-We have one of them.

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.501
Here's Michael Strahan's
game-winning touchdown.

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:28.367
-This was about
40 pounds ago, probably.

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:29.868
[ Laughter ]
-No.

00:02:29.868 --> 00:02:31.934
Here's your winning touchdown
against the Philadelphia Eagles

00:02:31.934 --> 00:02:34.968
in 1999.
Take a look at this.

00:02:34.968 --> 00:02:39.834
-From the 45 yard line,
third down play for the Eagles.

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:42.200
Peterson gets time.
The pass is blocked!

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.167
-Yeah! Go, Michael Strahan!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:44.167 --> 00:02:46.067
-Michael Strahan
headed for the end zone!

00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:48.267
-Yo!
-Strahan, touchdown!

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:49.334
And the Giants win!

00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:51.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:56.934
[ Discordant notes playing ]

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:59.400
-Stop it!
He's a guest!

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.934
He is a guest on this show!
Stop that!

00:03:01.934 --> 00:03:04.701
-[ Laughing ]
-He is a guest on our program!

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:06.868
-Okay.
I will be honest with you,

00:03:06.868 --> 00:03:08.767
it's great to score a touchdown.
-Yeah.

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:10.734
-I mean, it's great.
But when you do it against

00:03:10.734 --> 00:03:12.868
the Eagles, in overtime,
in Philly,

00:03:12.868 --> 00:03:14.834
and I can look y'all in the face
when it's going on...

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:16.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.234
...it's the best ever!

00:03:18.234 --> 00:03:22.934
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:25.934
-What I like is -- You know,
if you score a touchdown,

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:27.834
everyone's got to move.
They got different things.

00:03:27.834 --> 00:03:30.334
If you spike it or you could do
a dance or do something.

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:32.534
You just kind of went like...
-Oh!

00:03:32.534 --> 00:03:34.634
There was --
No, there was a reason.

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:37.033
Because the Friday
before that Sunday game,

00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:38.567
I went to see Ricky Martin.

00:03:38.567 --> 00:03:40.100
Me and some of my --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.767
Me and my teammates, we went to
see Ricky Martin at the Garden.

00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:44.400
-Yeah.
-And Ricky would come out,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.601
and before he even did anything,
he would just go like this.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:49.033
And the crowd was like, "Wow!"

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:50.234
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:53.133
So I said,
"If I score a touchdown,

00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:55.067
I'm gonna hit them
with the Ricky Martin."

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:56.200
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.634
And so I scored that --

00:03:57.634 --> 00:03:59.267
I had never thought
I was gonna score a touchdown.

00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:00.934
It happened that,
two days later,

00:04:00.934 --> 00:04:02.567
and I hit them
with a Ricky Martin.

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:06.133
-Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:07.501
That's a great story.
-Hey.

00:04:07.501 --> 00:04:09.367
-But the thing about Philly,
they were hitting me

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:10.801
with, like, stuff
they spit in the cups,

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:12.167
and they'd throw -- yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:14.734
So, they hit me back.
-Yeah, they got you back.

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:17.267
Can we talk about this
new podcast you're doing here?

00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:18.834
-Oh, yeah.
-"American Football:

00:04:18.834 --> 00:04:21.501
How the Gridiron was Forged."

00:04:21.501 --> 00:04:24.100
Kate Mara.
Talk about this.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.334
This is how football started,
the whole history?

00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:28.067
-Yeah.
It's the history of football.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:30.200
Like, I love football,
and I thought I knew

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.267
about the history of the game,
but this is

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.968
the real history of the game,
how it started.

00:04:34.968 --> 00:04:37.901
Football used to be,
like, white-collar,

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:40.200
and then the blue-collar teams
wanted to play, and they said,

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.334
"Nope, you can't play."

00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:43.033
So they kind of developed
their own leagues.

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:44.400
And it's just
a history of all that.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.167
And, also,
we have modern-day broadcasters,

00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:48.834
from Joe Buck to Curt Menefee,

00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:51.334
they are calling
some of the older games,

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:54.400
like narrating some of the older
games, which is really cool.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.267
And Kate Mara, what a lot
of people may not know,

00:04:57.267 --> 00:04:58.501
is a dear friend.

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:00.501
I've known her
since she was a kid --

00:05:00.501 --> 00:05:02.400
who now has kids
and was pregnant

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.367
and just had a kid last week
in the booth doing this.

00:05:05.367 --> 00:05:08.534
-Did she, really?
-She is a part of two dynasties.

00:05:08.534 --> 00:05:11.334
The Pittsburgh Steelers
and the New York Giants.

00:05:11.334 --> 00:05:12.901
Those are her parents.
-That's right.

00:05:12.901 --> 00:05:14.334
-So she is a part
of two dynasties

00:05:14.334 --> 00:05:16.434
and the perfect partner
to narrate this for us.

00:05:16.434 --> 00:05:18.701
She knocks it out of the water.

00:05:18.701 --> 00:05:20.634
-Talk about fun projects
to work on.

00:05:20.634 --> 00:05:21.901
You told me about this
last time.

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:23.133
You were like,
"We can't mention it yet."

00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:24.400
-Yeah, couldn't mention it.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.467
-Yeah, because this is
top-secret stuff.

00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:28.267
Your own skincare line.

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:30.133
-See, that's why
I don't look 51.

00:05:30.133 --> 00:05:34.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.133
-That's the secret.

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:38.467
That's the secret.
You moisturize.

00:05:38.467 --> 00:05:42.067
-I moisturize, man.
You know, as men,

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:43.367
doing this job,

00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:44.801
you do have to wear a little
makeup here and there.

00:05:44.801 --> 00:05:46.067
-Of course.
-I just consider mine to be

00:05:46.067 --> 00:05:47.801
anti-shine, not really makeup.

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.234
[ Laughter ]
But -- But --

00:05:49.234 --> 00:05:50.701
-Oh, my gosh.
-You know, and I think we just

00:05:50.701 --> 00:05:52.000
go through the grit
and the grime of the day,

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.567
and we run around.
And for men, it seems

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:55.601
inconvenient
to take care of your skin.

00:05:55.601 --> 00:05:58.367
But to me, like, wearing
a nice outfit, it's confidence.

00:05:58.367 --> 00:06:00.634
Taking care of your skin
is confidence.

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:03.634
So we developed this system,
and it's very easy to do.

00:06:03.634 --> 00:06:05.601
The face wash
and the moisturizer.

00:06:05.601 --> 00:06:09.634
Then there's a beard oil
and a shaving gel.

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:12.067
-What do you do with beard oil?
-Beard oil.

00:06:12.067 --> 00:06:14.934
Yeah, hey, by the way,
this is a great beard.

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:16.033
Y'all like his beard?

00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:17.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:19.234
-I'm in the beard game.

00:06:19.234 --> 00:06:21.601
I just --
I haven't seen you since --

00:06:21.601 --> 00:06:23.634
-Do you use beard oil?

00:06:23.634 --> 00:06:24.968
-65-years-old, by the way.

00:06:24.968 --> 00:06:26.100
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.033
-But do you use beard oil?

00:06:28.033 --> 00:06:29.534
-No, I have no idea
what beard oil is at all.

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:30.868
-Come here.
Let me see something.

00:06:30.868 --> 00:06:32.334
Oh, very coarse.

00:06:32.334 --> 00:06:33.701
Well, your beard
is pretty coarse.

00:06:33.701 --> 00:06:35.801
-Yeah, it's rough, right?
-Yeah, like a horse.

00:06:35.801 --> 00:06:38.467
[ Laughter ]

00:06:38.467 --> 00:06:40.000
-Doesn't feel like a horse!

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.334
-But seriously,
you should use beard oil.

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:44.701
-What would I do?
How would I use beard oil?

00:06:44.701 --> 00:06:45.901
-You got a little thing here.

00:06:45.901 --> 00:06:48.067
You just put a little few drops
right there.

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:49.801
-Yeah.
-Yeah, come here.

00:06:49.801 --> 00:06:51.667
You know, I'm gonna give you
star treatment.

00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:56.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:56.234 --> 00:06:58.033
-Wow!
Whoa.

00:06:58.033 --> 00:06:59.400
Wow.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.334
Oh, yeah.
Oh, yeah. Oh!

00:07:01.334 --> 00:07:03.467
Oh, I feel like a million bucks.

00:07:03.467 --> 00:07:04.634
Look at this.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:04.634 --> 00:07:08.200
Michael Strahan,
Daily Defense Beard Oil.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.868
That's what I'm talking about!
-That simple.

00:07:09.868 --> 00:07:10.968
-I feel good!

00:07:10.968 --> 00:07:13.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:13.734 --> 00:07:15.200
-I don't even --
I don't even need it.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:17.834
-You don't.
-We got some oils in there.

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:19.934
You can just rub it into
your cuticles, actually.

00:07:19.934 --> 00:07:21.501
-See, that's
what I'm talking about.

00:07:21.501 --> 00:07:22.834
He knows
what he's talking about.

00:07:22.834 --> 00:07:24.701
-Multitasking.
-Multitasking.

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:28.033
Michael Strahan, everybody.
Michael Strahan Daily Defense

00:07:28.033 --> 00:07:32.901
is available now on Amazon
and MichaelStrahanSkin.com.

00:07:32.901 --> 00:07:34.634
Michael and I are doing
something fun after the break.

00:07:34.634 --> 00:07:35.801
Stick around.

